Return the stored PPI priority, and deliver queued interrupts at the guest's priority.

Two defects in the same register path.  A 32-bit read of GICR_IPRIORITYR4-7
assigned the PPI priority array from *val, the output pointer, instead of
loading *val from it.  A guest reading a PPI priority back therefore overwrote
the stored priority with whatever its destination register held, and the next
injection used the corrupted value.  The self-test does not read a PPI
priority, so only a Windows HAL reading timer priorities would have found it.

The drain path also injected a queued interrupt at the priority its producer
stored: 0x20 for the timers and SGIs, 0x40 for a refused SPI.  Those are
placeholders, not the guest's priority, and an SGI/PPI priority is per-PE, so
a queue filled on one CPU is not authoritative on another.  The drain runs on
the CPU that will deliver the interrupt, so it reads the priority there.  This
changes preemption only on the queued path, which is the path the enable gate
introduced.

diff --git a/src/hv_vgic.c b/src/hv_vgic.c
--- a/src/hv_vgic.c
+++ b/src/hv_vgic.c
@@ -1525,8 +1525,16 @@
                 // u32 reg_num;
                 reg_num = (relative_addr - GIC_REDIST_IPRIORITYR4) / 4;
                 reg_offset = (relative_addr - GIC_REDIST_IPRIORITYR4) % 4;
+                //
+                // This is the read path, so the register has to be the source.
+                // It used to assign the array from *val here, which overwrote
+                // the stored PPI priority with the read buffer on every aligned
+                // access; a guest reading a PPI priority back got whatever its
+                // destination register happened to hold, and the next injection
+                // used the corrupted priority.
+                //
                 if(reg_offset == 0)
-                    redistributors[cpu_num].sgi_region.gicr_ppi_ipriority_reg[reg_num] = *val;
+                    *val = redistributors[cpu_num].sgi_region.gicr_ppi_ipriority_reg[reg_num];
                 else{
                     //TODO: handle width
                     u8 *reg_u8 = (u8 *)&redistributors[cpu_num].sgi_region.gicr_ppi_ipriority_reg[reg_num];
diff --git a/src/hv_exc.c b/src/hv_exc.c
--- a/src/hv_exc.c
+++ b/src/hv_exc.c
@@ -99,7 +99,17 @@
         if (hv_vgic3_irq_enabled(pending.vintid) &&
             hv_vgic3_inject_irq(
                 pending.vintid,
-                pending.priority,
+                // 
+                // Take the priority from the guest's current register state,
+                // not from the entry.  The producers queue a placeholder (0x20
+                // for the timers and SGIs, 0x40 for a refused SPI) because the
+                // interrupt is queued on the CPU that took the physical one,
+                // and an SGI/PPI priority is per-PE.  Injecting the entry's
+                // value delivered a queued interrupt at a priority the guest
+                // never programmed, which changes preemption on the drained
+                // path but not on the immediate one.
+                //
+                hv_vgic3_get_priority(pending.vintid),
                 pending.active,
                 pending.pending,
                 pending.hw_status,
